A||||9||Indy-2M4||Server Problems||||1||
Z||000000||Indy-2M4||12-25-2001||12:34 PM||||Okay, I see a lot of you guys are setting up your own servers, and I have been working on doing the same. I am using OmniHttp Proffessiional. Now, the real problem I am having is with the dynamic IP address. I guess everytime my line drops and I log back on to the net, my server IP address changes. Does anyone know how to resolve this problem? Perhaps how I can get a static IP (perminent?) address? I'm new at this server thing and want to be able to host my own Fiero website. The host I am using now has now changed to pay hosting. The free service is just to limiting. <P>Here is a test run of the server now...http://216.143.148.70/Welcome%20to%20S_O_F_A.htm  ,well at least until I get knocked off line again! Thanks in advance for your help.<P>------------------<BR>Kathy<BR>http:\\sofa.iwarp.com<BR>    <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/IndyCrueSig.jpg">   <P>[This message has been edited by Indy-2M4 (edited 12-25-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Indy-2M4 (edited 12-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indy-2M4||12-25-2001||12:49 PM||||Uhhhh, I think I just found an answer to my IP question in my settings. I'm gonna play around with this and see if it works.||||reg||1
Z||000002||LarryB||12-25-2001||01:30 PM||||Talk to your service provider and see if you can upgrade to static IP addresses.  If you were using a service like AT&T Broadband that assigns a unique machine name in their DNS maps to each customers machine, with long retention periods for the dynamic IP address assigned, you could get an alias in someone else's DNS maps (by using what is known as a Canonical Name Record, aka CNAME record), but your provider (broadband.net) assigns addresses to ports, not machines, so this technique wouldn't be practical.<P>To see an example of exactly what I'm talking about, do a name lookup of killer.xcom.net.  Or, at least do a ping and see the actual machine name returned. <P>Does Broadwing provide free web space on their servers to their customers non-commercial use and would that fit your needs? <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||12-25-2001||01:30 PM||||Depending on the network you are on, a Static IP will not be a Good Thing.<P>If the network you are on is set to DHCP (dynamic addressing) and you try to override the address, you can cause problems. Firstly, if your machine is off, and misses a "DHCP-renew" then the address will be reassigned. That means both you and another machine will have the same address. Both systems will CHOKE. Talk to your provider before playing with those settings. You SHOULD be able to setup the server with domain-naming, although the address will change...||||reg||4
Z||000004||LarryB||12-25-2001||01:43 PM||||Won't work out of the box.  Unlike AT&T, Broadwing (and UUnet, and Level3) by default maps their pool of DNS and reverse-resolve names to the dialup ports, not the MAC addresses of the customer's network cards. The odds of getting the exact same port when dialing back in are extremely small; the POPs of the larger bulk dialup providers in the big metro areas can have over 100,000 available dialup lines. <P>Every time you rebooted the server the DNS map would need to be updated and recent vistors would have trouble revisiting until the local DNS caches flushed and were reloaded. <P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>You SHOULD be able to setup the server with domain-naming, although the address will change...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><p>[This message has been edited by LarryB (edited 12-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||LarryB||12-25-2001||01:47 PM||||Heh, goofed up edit. :-/<p>[This message has been edited by LarryB (edited 12-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Indy-2M4||12-25-2001||03:56 PM||||Arrrgggh, yeah, I see what you guys mean. Getting in and playing with those TCP/IP settings didn't do anything. So, basicaly what you guys are saying is that anytime I get my ISP and get back on, the IP will always change? How else (if I can) can I get around this? Thanx again.||||reg||1
Z||000007||LarryB||12-25-2001||07:34 PM||||Yes. Every time you dial up you're virtually guaranteed to have a different IP address. <P>See if your provider (or an alternate provider) can offer you a dedicated dialup line with DHCP addressing using a long lease or a static IP address, and optionally some kind of vanity naming e.g. "indy-2m4.broadwing.net". Don't be surprised if there's an additional monthly charge for this. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||watts||12-25-2001||11:49 PM||||Go check out <A HREF="http://www.dhs.org" TARGET=_blank>www.dhs.org</A> <P>You get to pick a URL (like fred.dhs.org), then you run a small program at boot time that shoots your new IP back to the guys at DHS.org everytime your machine reboots.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Indy-2M4||12-26-2001||06:25 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by watts:<BR><B>Go check out <A HREF="http://www.dhs.org" TARGET=_blank>www.dhs.org</A> <P>You get to pick a URL (like fred.dhs.org), then you run a small program at boot time that shoots your new IP back to the guys at DHS.org everytime your machine reboots.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think that is just what I am looking for. Thank all of you guys for your help. I'll keep you posted on how it turns out. Time is kinda limited now.<P>||||reg||1
